{"cuisine/venetian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/venetian-cuisine","n":"Venetian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["Venetian cuisine, from the Veneto region, has a centuries-long history and differs significantly from other cuisines of northern Italy (notably Friuli-Venezia Giulia and Trentino-Alto Adige/Südtirol), and of neighbouring Austria and of Slavic countries (notably Slovenia and Croatia), despite sharing some commonalities."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"venetian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venetian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Sarde in saor","dish/sarde-in-saor"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Polpo: A Venetian Cookbook (of Sorts)","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Russell Norman, 2012","note":"Venetian bacaro dishes from the London restaurateur behind Polpo.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781408816790","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781408816790"},{"name":"Venice: Four Seasons of Home Cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Skye McAlpine","note":"Seasonal Venetian home cooking from a writer who grew up in Venice.","url":"https://www.penguin.com.au/books/venice-9780241299920","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780241299920"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["European pilchard","ingredient/european-pilchard"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/1e/Sarde_in_sa%C3%B2r.jpg/500px-Sarde_in_sa%C3%B2r.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"ezioman","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarde_in_sa%C3%B2r.jpg","of":"Sarde in saor"}},"cuisine/cuisine-of-pembrokeshire":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-pembrokeshire","n":"Cuisine of Pembrokeshire","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Welsh cuisine","cuisine/welsh-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United Kingdom","intro":[["Pembrokeshire has been called \"the cottage garden of Wales\", due to its good soil and the beneficial effects of the Gulf Stream, which provide a mild climate and a longer growing season than other parts of the country."],["The good climate and soil meant that the south of the peninsula was coveted by the Norsemen and Normans because it had \"great plentie\" of corn and cattle The county has prime agricultural land, much of which is located at about 70m above sea level, while to the north, the Preseli Hills rise to 500m above sea level and form uplands that are made up of heather and bracken, which are used for grazing sheep."],["Consequently, Pembrokeshire is classed as one of the most fertile counties in Wales, with its 392,300 agricultural acres having 14% of its land classed as of good quality, 67% being classed as medium quality and 19% being classed as poor quality. However, agricultural production is subject to market forces and in the 1890s, as a result of the Panic of 1893, a deep agricultural depression led to the area under cultivation falling by a third. Many labourers and farmers had no option but to emigrate to the New World and many of the large farming estates were sold. World War I brought prosperity again, but by the 1930s, as a result of the Great Depression, there was another agricultural depression which lasted until World War II. During the Post-war period agriculture has benefited from marketing schemes and marketing boards, which have helped in the regulation, marketing and distribution of the county's agricultural production."],["Today, the county is well known for its excellent food, having capitalised on the quality of its agricultural produce. Gilli Davies notes that Fishguard is known as a mecca for the gourmet, while St Davids has developed a reputation in recent years as a centre for good food shops and Milford Haven is known for its range of local foods and delicatessen items."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-pembrokeshire"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Pembrokeshire"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Welsh cuisine","cuisine/welsh-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Welsh cuisine","cuisine/welsh-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d1/Waiting_for_the_judging_at_Fishguard_show_-_geograph.org.uk_-_307543.jpg/500px-Waiting_for_the_judging_at_Fishguard_show_-_geograph.org.uk_-_307543.jpg","w":500,"h":391,"by":"ceridwen","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waiting_for_the_judging_at_Fishguard_show_-_geograph.org.uk_-_307543.jpg"}},"ingredient/garam-masala":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/garam-masala","n":"Garam masala","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Garam masala is a blend of ground spices originating in the Indian Subcontinent. It is common in Indian, Pakistani, Nepalese, and Bangladeshi cuisines. It is used alone or with other seasonings. The specific combination differs by region, but it regularly incorporates a blend of flavours like cardamom, cinnamon, cumin, cloves, bay leaves, star anise, and peppercorns. Garam masala is used in a wide range of dishes, including marinades, pickles, stews, and curries."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-garam-masala"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garam_masala"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Baingan bharta","dish/baingan-bharta"],["Butter chicken","dish/butter-chicken"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Brinjal","ingredient/brinjal"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Cilantro","ingredient/cilantro"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Cumin","ingredient/cumin"],["Green peas","ingredient/green-peas"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Kasuri methi","ingredient/kasuri-methi"],["Tomatoes","ingredient/tomatoes"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/58/Garammasalaphoto.jpg/500px-Garammasalaphoto.jpg","w":500,"h":373,"by":"Badagnani","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garammasalaphoto.jpg"}},"ingredient/bean":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/bean","n":"Bean","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 6 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["A bean is the seed of plants in many genera of the legume family (Fabaceae) used as a vegetable for human consumption or animal feed. The seeds are sold fresh or preserved through drying (a pulse). Beans have been cultivated since the seventh millennium BCE in Thailand, and since the second millennium BCE in Europe and in Peru. Most beans, with the exception of peas, are summer crops. As legumes, the plants fix nitrogen and form seeds with a high protein content. They are produced on a scale of millions of tons annually in many countries, with India as the largest producer."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-bean"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bean"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"]]],["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Lobiani","dish/lobiani"]]],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine",[["Eintopf","dish/eintopf"]]],["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",[["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"]]],["Serbian cuisine","cuisine/serbian-cuisine",[["Pasulj","dish/pasulj"]]],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia",[["Eintopf","dish/eintopf"]]],["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",[["Piyaz","dish/piyaz"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Beans, dry","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":29765208,"top":[["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",7880613],["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",2945451],["Myanmar","cuisine/burmese-cuisine",2629516],["Tanzania","cuisine/tanzanian-cuisine",1355270],["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",1322710],["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",1222307],["Mexico","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",1006057],["Kenya","cuisine/kenyan-cuisine",791645],["Uganda","cuisine/ugandan-cuisine",788425],["Ethiopia","cuisine/ethiopian-cuisine",784771],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",667929],["Burundi","cuisine/burundian-cuisine",492940],["Mozambique","cuisine/mozambican-cuisine",477889],["Rwanda","cuisine/rwandan-cuisine",461773],["Canada","cuisine/canadian-cuisine",385905]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Phaseolus","ingredient/phaseolus"],["Game meat","ingredient/game-meat"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Adobada","ingredient/adobada"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Flour tortilla","ingredient/flour-tortilla"],["Jalapeño","ingredient/jalapeno"],["Machaca","ingredient/machaca"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tortilla","ingredient/tortilla"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Beer","ingredient/beer"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"]]},{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Three Sisters","ingredient/three-sisters-agriculture"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a0/French_beans_J1.JPG/500px-French_beans_J1.JPG","w":500,"h":682,"by":"Jamain","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_beans_J1.JPG"}},"dish/cotoletta-alla-bolognese":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/cotoletta-alla-bolognese","n":"Cotoletta alla bolognese","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Cotoletta alla bolognese is a variety of cotoletta (veal cutlet preparation) from the city of Bologna, Italy. It is also known as petroniana, after Petronius, a fifth century bishop and the patron saint of Bologna."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cotoletta-alla-bolognese"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotoletta_alla_bolognese"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Ham","ingredient/ham"],["Parmesan","ingredient/parmesan"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pasta Grannies","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pastagrannies.substack.com","note":"Vicky Bennison's long-running video series filming Italian grandmothers making regional pasta by hand.","url":"https://pastagrannies.substack.com/p/pasta-grannies-is-now-on-substack","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Recipes from Italy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"recipesfromitaly.com","note":"Traditional Italian recipes written by Italian home cooks, organized by region and course.","url":"https://recipesfromitaly.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Manu's Menu","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"manusmenu.com","note":"Italian-born home cook Manuela sharing classic family and regional Italian dishes.","url":"https://www.manusmenu.com/about/about-this-site","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Italian cuisine","links":[["Acquacotta","dish/acquacotta"],["Agliata","dish/agliata"],["Agnolotti","dish/agnolotti"],["Antipasto","dish/antipasto"],["Arancini","dish/arancini"],["Biscotti","dish/biscotti"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bombolone","dish/bombolone"],["Budino","dish/budino"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Cannelloni","dish/cannelloni"],["Capellini","dish/capellini"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Carpaccio","dish/carpaccio"],["Cassatella di sant'Agata","dish/cassatella-di-sant-agata"],["Cassoeula","dish/cassoeula"],["Ciabatta","dish/ciabatta"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Coda alla vaccinara","dish/coda-alla-vaccinara"],["Colomba pasquale","dish/colomba-pasquale"],["Conti roll","dish/conti-roll"],["Crostata","dish/crostata"],["Drago restaurants","dish/drago-restaurants"],["Focaccia pugliese","dish/focaccia-pugliese"],["Frittata","dish/frittata"],["Giardiniera","dish/giardiniera"],["Guanciale","dish/guanciale"],["Italian restaurant","dish/italian-restaurant"],["Italian wedding soup","dish/italian-wedding-soup"],["Lampredotto","dish/lampredotto"],["Lasagna","dish/lasagna"],["List of Italian restaurants","dish/list-of-italian-restaurants"],["Marinara sauce","dish/marinara-sauce"],["Mondeghili","dish/mondeghili"],["Paccheri","dish/paccheri"],["Panini (sandwich)","dish/panini-sandwich"],["Panna cotta","dish/panna-cotta"],["Passatelli","dish/passatelli"],["Pecorino","dish/pecorino"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f9/Cotoletta_alla_bolognese.jpg/500px-Cotoletta_alla_bolognese.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"WILO-MA","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cotoletta_alla_bolognese.jpg"}},"ingredient/lathyrus-sativus":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/lathyrus-sativus","n":"Lathyrus sativus","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Lathyrus sativus, also known as grass pea, grasspea, peagrass, cicerchia, blue sweet pea, chickling pea, chickling vetch, Indian pea, white pea and white vetch, is a legume (family Fabaceae) commonly grown for human consumption and livestock feed in Asia and East Africa. It is a particularly important crop in areas that are prone to drought and famine, and is thought of as an 'insurance crop' as it produces reliable yields when all other crops fail. The seeds contain a neurotoxin that causes lathyrism, a neurodegenerative disease, if eaten as a primary protein source for a prolonged period."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lathyrus-sativus"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lathyrus_sativus"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Andalusian cuisine","cuisine/andalusian-cuisine",[["Gachas","dish/gachas"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ae/Lathyrus_sativus_flowers_Bangladesh_cropped.JPG/500px-Lathyrus_sativus_flowers_Bangladesh_cropped.JPG","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Arr4 (talk)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lathyrus_sativus_flowers_Bangladesh_cropped.JPG"}},"dish/jesuite":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/jesuite","n":"Jésuite","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Argentine cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A Jésuite is a triangular, Puff pastry filled with frangipane cream and topped with sliced almonds and powdered sugar. The pastry originated in France and the name refers to the triangular shape of a Jesuit's hat. A similarly-named sweet pastry known in Portugal and Spain, the jesuíta, consists of puff pastry filled with custard. In Germany, Jesuitermützen are a custard-filled pastry traditionally cut into triangles."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-jesuite"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J%C3%A9suite"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Argentine cuisine","cuisine/argentine-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Frangipane","ingredient/frangipane"],["Powdered sugar","ingredient/powdered-sugar"],["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Familia Kitchen: Argentina","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"familiakitchen.com","note":"Argentine recipes and stories on a Latino family cooking site.","url":"https://familiakitchen.com/destination/argentina/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Argentine cuisine","links":[["Anticucho","dish/anticucho"],["Argentine pizza","dish/argentine-pizza"],["Asado","dish/asado"],["Bara brith","dish/bara-brith"],["Bondiola sandwich","dish/bondiola-sandwich"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Dulce de batata","dish/dulce-de-batata"],["Fugazza","dish/fugazza"],["Jesuita (sandwich)","dish/jesuita-sandwich"],["Matambre","dish/matambre"],["Puchero","dish/puchero"],["Rogel (cake)","dish/rogel-cake"],["Salsa golf","dish/salsa-golf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c9/J%C3%A9suite_%288090911085%29.jpg/500px-J%C3%A9suite_%288090911085%29.jpg","w":500,"h":229,"by":"Stijn Nieuwendijk from Amsterdam, NL","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J%C3%A9suite_(8090911085).jpg"}},"dish/khao-soi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/khao-soi","n":"Khao soi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Khao soi or khao soy is a dish served in Northern Thailand. The dish is believed to have evolved from Chin Haw Muslim traders who plied the spice route when what is now modern-day northern Thailand was controlled by the Burmese. A comparable dish, ohn no khao swè, is widely served in Myanmar, most likely the precursor of khao soi. Traditionally, the dough for the wheat noodles is spread out on a cloth stretched over boiling water. After steaming, the sheet noodles are rolled and cut with scissors."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-khao-soi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khao_soi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Burmese cuisine","cuisine/burmese-cuisine"],["Lao cuisine","cuisine/lao-cuisine"],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Saeng's Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"saengskitchen.com","note":"Lao-American cook Saeng Douangdara's recipes and videos of Lao home cooking.","url":"https://www.saengskitchen.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Hot Thai Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hot-thai-kitchen.com","note":"Pailin Chongchitnant's recipes and videos, with background on technique and ingredients.","url":"https://hot-thai-kitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"SheSimmers","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"shesimmers.com","note":"Bangkok-born writer Leela Punyaratabandhu's blog on Thai home cooking.","url":"https://shesimmers.com/who-is-shesimmers","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Burmese cuisine","links":[["Burmese salads","dish/burmese-salads"],["Mohinga","dish/mohinga"],["Thali","dish/thali"],["Thua nao","dish/thua-nao"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9f/Chiang_Mai_-_Khao_Soi_Khun_Yai_-_%E0%B8%82%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%8B%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%93%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A2_%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%88_%282026%29_-_IMG_03.jpg/500px-Chiang_Mai_-_Khao_Soi_Khun_Yai_-_%E0%B8%82%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%8B%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%93%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A2_%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%88_%282026%29_-_IMG_03.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Chainwit.","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiang_Mai_-_Khao_Soi_Khun_Yai_-_%E0%B8%82%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%8B%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%93%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A2_%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%88_(2026)_-_IMG_03.jpg"}},"ingredient/silver-stripe-round-herring":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/silver-stripe-round-herring","n":"Silver-stripe round herring","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The silver-stripe round herring, slender sprat, or Kibinago minnow (Spratelloides gracilis) is a small, herring-like forage fish. They are small fish used as fishing bait, especially in skipjack tuna-fishing. It is valued as food in Japan, where it is known as kibinago. These can be eaten raw, as sashimi, or cooked, as whitebait."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-silver-stripe-round-herring"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver-stripe_round_herring"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Malay cuisine","cuisine/malay-cuisine",[["Lekor","dish/lekor"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Rastrelliger kanagurta","ingredient/rastrelliger-kanagurta"],["Round sardinella","ingredient/round-sardinella"],["Sago","ingredient/sago"],["Tapioca starch","ingredient/tapioca-starch"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8c/XRF-Spratelloides_gracilis.jpg/500px-XRF-Spratelloides_gracilis.jpg","w":500,"h":248,"by":"Xavier Romero-Frias","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:XRF-Spratelloides_gracilis.jpg"}},"dish/mianwo":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/mianwo","n":"Mianwo","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Mianwo is a deep-fried, donut-shaped snack from Wuhan in Hubei province in central China. It is made from soy milk, rice milk, flour, sesame, and chopped Chinese scallion. Mianwo usually has a salty taste, but a sweeter variant may be made with diced sweet potato."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-mianwo"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mianwo"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["Hubei cuisine","cuisine/hubei-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Allium fistulosum","ingredient/allium-fistulosum"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Sesame","ingredient/sesame"],["Soybean","ingredient/soybean"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thewoksoflife.com","note":"Chinese and Chinese American recipes from the Leung family, running since 2013.","url":"https://thewoksoflife.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Chinese Cooking Demystified","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"youtube.com","note":"Technique-heavy YouTube channel on regional Chinese home and restaurant cooking.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@ChineseCookingDemystified","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Red House Spice","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"redhousespice.com","note":"Classic Chinese recipes from Wei Guo, who grew up in Gansu, blogging since 2017.","url":"https://redhousespice.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Madame Huang's Kitchen: Henan cuisine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"carolynjphillips.blogspot.com","note":"Recipe posts on Hubei dishes from Carolyn Phillips' blog, Madame Huang's Kitchen.","url":"https://carolynjphillips.blogspot.com/search/label/Hubei%20cuisine","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Chinese cuisine","links":[["Almond biscuit","dish/almond-biscuit"],["Banana roll","dish/banana-roll"],["Baozi","dish/baozi"],["Bear paw","dish/bear-paw"],["Black sesame roll","dish/black-sesame-roll"],["Century egg","dish/century-egg"],["Cha siu bao","dish/cha-siu-bao"],["Chinese restaurant","dish/ch